# Force LF everywhere, regardless of core.autocrlf on any given
# machine. Without this, Windows' common core.autocrlf=true setting
# rewrites some tracked files to CRLF on checkout - invisible to `git
# status` (which normalizes the comparison) but a real byte-level
# difference to anything that hashes the file as-is, like
# tools/publish_supabase.py's drift check. That caused the same handful
# of files to keep re-appearing as "changed" on every branch/merge with
# no actual content change - this setting removes the platform
# dependency that let it happen at all.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets - never touched by line-ending conversion regardless of
# the rule above, but explicit is safer than relying on Git's own
# content-sniffing to guess right for every current and future file.
*.png binary
*.mp4 binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary
*.ico binary
